<root>
  <problem>
    <description><![CDATA[
      Given N elves in a circle, repeatedly remove the elf who is targeted by the stealing rule until one elf remains. Part 1 steals from the left neighbor; Part 2 steals from the elf directly across the circle (with tie-breaking to the left from the stealer’s perspective). Determine which starting position ends up with all presents.
    ]]></description>
    <pseudocode>
      # We will solve the “across the circle” version (Part 2) using a direct formula.
      # Input: N
      # Output: position of the winning elf (1-indexed)

      function winner_across_circle(N):
          if N == 1:
              return 1

          # Let N = 2^k + m where 2^k is the largest power of 2 <= N
          k = floor(log2(N))
          p = 2^k
          m = N - p

          # For the across-circle Josephus variant:
          # - If N is exactly a power of 2, the winner is position N.
          # - Otherwise, the winner is m*2 + 1.
          if m == 0:
              return N
          else:
              return 2*m + 1

      read N from standard input
      print winner_across_circle(N)
    </pseudocode>
    <techniques><![CDATA[
      <technique>Josephus-style elimination modeled as a closed-form (math/bit manipulation) rather than simulation.</technique>
      <technique>Use of largest power of two ≤ N via floor(log2) / bit operations.</technique>
      <technique>Case analysis based on whether N is a power of two.</technique>
    ]]></techniques>
    <planning><![CDATA[
