      3. Compute p = highest power of 2 not exceeding N (p = 2^⌊log2 N⌋).
      4. Let m = N - p.
      5. Apply the rule:
         - If m = 0 (N is a power of two), winner = N.
         - Else winner = 2*m + 1.
      6. Output the winner as a 1-indexed position.
      7. Efficiency:
         - Time: O(1) arithmetic.
         - Space: O(1).
    ]]></planning>
  </problem>

  <problem>
    <description><![CDATA[
      (Related) Josephus Problem (classic): N people in a circle; every k-th person is eliminated; find the last remaining position.
    ]]></description>
    <pseudocode>
      # Step-by-step dynamic recurrence (generic Josephus)
      function josephus(N, k):
          # Base case: with 1 person, winner is position 1
          winner = 1
          for i from 2 to N:
              # When size grows from i-1 to i, shift by k and wrap around
              winner = ((winner + k - 1) mod i) + 1
          return winner

      read N, k
      print josephus(N, k)
    </pseudocode>
    <techniques><![CDATA[
      <technique>Dynamic programming via recurrence: J(i) = (J(i-1)+k) mod i.</technique>
      <technique>Modular arithmetic for circular indexing.</technique>
      <technique>Iterative DP to avoid recursion depth issues.</technique>
    ]]></techniques>
    <planning><![CDATA[
      1. Model the elimination as a recurrence on the number of remaining people.
      2. Use the known Josephus recurrence:
         - Let J(i) be the winner position among i people (1-indexed).
         - J(1)=1.
         - J(i) = ((J(i-1) + k - 1) mod i) + 1.
      3. Compute iteratively from i=2..N.
      4. Complexity:
         - Time: O(N)
         - Space: O(1)
      5. Use this approach when no special closed-form is known for the specific k/step pattern.
    ]]></planning>
  </problem>

